# Salem Street Burying Ground page; Brooks and the younger Dr. Simon (owner requests 2026-11-03).
MHR33 = '&ldquo;Medford&rsquo;s Tercentenary Observances,&rdquo; <em>Medford Historical Register</em>, vol. XXXIII, nos. 1&ndash;2 (March&ndash;June 1930), pp. 1&ndash;2. <a href="https://archive.org/stream/medfordhistorica08medf/medfordhistorica08medf_djvu.txt">Internet Archive text</a> &middot; <a href="https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/public/gdcmassbookdig/medfordhistorica08medf/medfordhistorica08medf_bw.pdf">Library of Congress scan (PDF)</a>'
MHR3C2 = PU("2005.05.0002", 2)
SSBG = f'''<section id="about"><p>Medford&rsquo;s oldest burial ground, on Salem Street, holds the elder and younger Drs. Simon Tufts and many of the town&rsquo;s Revolutionary generation. In Medford&rsquo;s tercentenary year, 1930, the city rebuilt its wall and gate and set two bronze tablets on the gate pillars.</p>
{TA('Burial place of <a href="{P}profiles/simon-tufts-1700.html">Dr. Simon Tufts (1700&ndash;1747)</a> and <a href="{P}profiles/simon-tufts-11-1.html">Dr. Simon Tufts (1727&ndash;1786)</a>, plot 35; the 1930 gate tablet names an &ldquo;Honorable Peter Tufts.&rdquo;', '<a href="https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/26903835/simon-tufts">Find a Grave 26903835</a>, <a href="https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/26903836/simon-fms-tufts">26903836</a>; ' + MHR33)}</section>{{DIV}}
<section id="tablets"><h2>The 1930 Tercentenary Tablets</h2>
<p>The <em>Register</em> describes the Patriots&rsquo; Day exercises of 1930: &ldquo;The city had reconstructed the enclosing wall of brick and concrete with granite cap, with a well-designed iron gate at the entrance on Salem street. Bronze tablets had been placed upon its pillars.&rdquo; It then prints the two inscriptions, given here line by line as printed:</p>
<blockquote class="tablet">Y<sup>e</sup> OLDE<br>MEDFORD<br>BURYING GROUND<br>IN USE PRIOR TO<br>1683<br>THIS TABLET DEDICATED<br>APRIL 19, 1930<br>IN MEDFORD&rsquo;S<br>TERCENTENARY YEAR</blockquote>
<blockquote class="tablet">HERE LIE<br>EARLY SETTLERS OF MEDFORD<br>GOVERNOR JOHN BROOKS<br>CAPTAIN ISAAC HALL<br>HONORABLE PETER TUFTS<br>SARAH BRADLEE FULTON<br>PATRIOTS OF THE REVOLUTION<br>WHO FOUGHT AT LEXINGTON<br>AND BUNKER HILL<br>AND OTHERS WHOSE VIRTUES<br>THIS TABLET COMMEMORATES<br>DEDICATED<br>APRIL 19, 1930</blockquote>
<p class="cite">{MHR33}</p>
<p>The <em>Register</em> adds that a bronze tablet reproducing the old inscription was placed on the Governor Brooks monument, and a memorial flagstaff was set up near River Street.</p>
{UNV("Which Peter Tufts the tablet means. The Medford Historical Society and Freedom&rsquo;s Way identify the Peter buried here as Capt. Peter Tufts (1648&ndash;1721), father of the elder Dr. Simon. But Brooks (<em>History of Medford</em>, p. 484) gives Capt. Peter&rsquo;s gravestone as being &ldquo;in the graveyard at Malden.&rdquo; The tablet also does not say which of the four names it calls patriots of the Revolution. Capt. Peter died in 1721, long before the Revolution. This site has no profile for Capt. Peter.")}
</section>{{DIV}}
<section id="also"><h2>Also Here</h2><p>The burying ground was used &ldquo;as early as 1683&rdquo; and was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1981 (<a href="https://medfordhistorical.org/mapping-medford/walking-tours/medford-early-history/">Medford Historical Society</a>; <a href="https://freedomsway.org/place/salem-street-burying-ground/">Freedom&rsquo;s Way</a>). A 2019 student memorial marks the southwest corner, where more than fifty enslaved people were buried without recognition (Freedom&rsquo;s Way).</p></section>'''
page("salem-street-burying-ground.html", "Salem Street Burying Ground | Tufts Lineage &amp; Legacy", "Places &middot; Medford", "Salem Street Burying Ground", "Medford&rsquo;s oldest burial ground and its 1930 tablets", SSBG)
BRK = (f'<p>John Brooks (1752&ndash;1825), major of minutemen in 1775 and later governor of Massachusetts, was the apprentice of <a href="{{P}}profiles/simon-tufts-11-1.html">Dr. Simon Tufts the younger (1727&ndash;1786)</a>. Brooks&rsquo;s <em>History of Medford</em> says the doctor:</p>'
 + Q("was so interested in the virtuous character and thirst for knowledge of young John Brooks that he almost adopted him as a son. He took his pupil under a written indenture, as an apprentice for seven years, to learn the science and practice of medicine", BRP(306))
 + '<p>Brooks adds that the doctor put him to a classical school, took him into his family, and later invited him back to Medford and &ldquo;resigned to him his practice.&rdquo; In 1776 William Tudor wrote to John Adams that Major Brooks &ldquo;studied Physick with Doctor Tufts of Medford&rdquo; (<em>Papers of John Adams</em>, vol. 5, pp. 7&ndash;9. <a href="' + MHSV + 'ADMS-06-05-02-0003">MHS</a>).</p>'
 + Q("He was a Medford boy, and only two years before had left the home of Dr. Simon Tufts, where he was educated, to practice medicine in Reading. Probably some of the men had been drilled by him in school-boy days in the vacant lot back of the doctor&rsquo;s house.", f'<em>Medford Historical Register</em>, vol. III, p. 31, on the Medford minute company of April 19, 1775. <a href="{MHR3C2}">Perseus</a>')
 + '<p>The fullest account is Dr. John Dixwell&rsquo;s memoir of Brooks, which Charles Brooks quotes in his <em>History of Medford</em>. It describes the apprentice years in the younger doctor&rsquo;s household, which began when John Brooks was fourteen (about 1766, nearly twenty years after the elder Dr. Simon&rsquo;s death in 1747):</p>'
 + Q("In the hours of relaxation from study, he amused himself with the drill and exercise of the soldier. His manners were so gentle and attractive that he was the delight of all the village boys; they collected about him as the chief source of their pleasures and amusements; he formed them into a company, and trained and exercised them in all the duties of military discipline. Dr. Tuft&rsquo;s yard was often converted into a train-field, and displayed in miniature all &lsquo;the pomp and circumstance of glorious war.&rsquo;", 'Dr. John Dixwell, quoted in Charles Brooks, <em>History of the Town of Medford</em> (1855), p. 128 (the 1886 Usher edition reprints it, pp. 132&ndash;133). <a href="https://archive.org/stream/historyoftownofm1855broo/historyoftownofm1855broo_djvu.txt">Internet Archive text</a>')
 + '<p class="note">Note: this is the source for the family story of training at Dr. Tufts&rsquo;s. As printed, it describes a boys&rsquo; company drilled by the young apprentice in the doctor&rsquo;s yard, not the Medford minutemen, and it says &ldquo;yard,&rdquo; not farmhouse or farm. The <em>Register</em>&rsquo;s &ldquo;probably&rdquo; above is a later guess that some of those boys became the men of 1775.</p>'
 + UNV("That the Medford minute company of 1775 itself trained at Dr. Tufts&rsquo;s house or farm. No source found says so. Brooks&rsquo;s <em>History</em> (1855, p. 128) describes only the boys&rsquo; drills in the doctor&rsquo;s yard during Brooks&rsquo;s apprenticeship (about 1766&ndash;1773)."))
_EXTRA10 = EXTRA
def EXTRA(path):
    out = _EXTRA10(path)
    link = '{DIV}<section id="burying-ground"><h2>Salem Street Burying Ground</h2><p>%s See <a href="{P}salem-street-burying-ground.html">Salem Street Burying Ground</a> and its 1930 tablets.</p></section>'
    if path == "profiles/simon-tufts-1700.html": out += link % "Buried in plot 35."
    if path == "profiles/simon-tufts-11-1.html": out += '{DIV}<section id="john-brooks"><h2>His Apprentice, John Brooks</h2>' + BRK + '</section>' + link % "Buried in plot 35 with his father."
    if path == "profiles/peter-tufts-jr.html": out += link % "The 1930 gate tablet names an &ldquo;Honorable Peter Tufts.&rdquo; Which Peter that is has not been established, and it is probably not this Peter (1753&ndash;1832)."
    if path == "generations/revolution.html": out += '{DIV}<section id="brooks-tufts"><h2>Dr. Tufts and John Brooks</h2>' + BRK + '</section>'
    if path == "places.html": out = '{DIV}<section id="salem-street"><h2>Salem Street Burying Ground, Medford</h2><p>The burial place of both Drs. Simon Tufts, with its 1930 gate tablets. See <a href="{P}salem-street-burying-ground.html">Salem Street Burying Ground</a>.</p></section>' + out
    return out
